-- title:
--   (26)–(27) — an explicit element of $\partial g(x^{k+1})$ and of $\partial_L F(x^{k+1})$
-- statement:
--   Let $f,g,h,\varphi,L,l$ satisfy Assumption 1 with $h$ differentiable, let $\delta\in(0,1)$, let $(x^k)$ be generated by DCAe and $y^k$ the extrapolated points. Then for every $k$
--
--   $$
--   L\nabla\varphi(y^k)-\nabla f(y^k)+\nabla h(x^k)-L\nabla\varphi(x^{k+1})\in\partial g(x^{k+1}) \qquad (26)
--   $$
--
--   and
--
--   $$
--   L\bigl(\nabla\varphi(y^k)-\nabla\varphi(x^{k+1})\bigr)+\nabla f(x^{k+1})-\nabla f(y^k)+\nabla h(x^k)-\nabla h(x^{k+1})\in\partial_L F(x^{k+1}). \qquad (27)
--   $$
--
--   Here $\partial g$ is the convex subdifferential and $\partial_L$ the limiting subdifferential. The element (27) is the one whose norm the relative error condition (H2) bounds.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The limiting subdifferential is the published `NonconvexSplitting.Shared.LimitingSubdiff`.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: E a finite dimensional real linear space with inner product ⟨·,·⟩ and norm ‖·‖ → any finite-dimensional real inner product space E; reason: the paper uses ⟨·,·⟩ and ‖·‖ on E; same instances
--   - encoding: g : E → (−∞, +∞] and F = f + g − h extended-real → g and F valued in EReal; reason: Lean convention; f and h are real so no ∞ − ∞ occurs; same instances
--   - encoding: x^{−1} := x^0 (initialization of Algorithm 1) → x : ℕ → E and prev x 0 = x 0, prev x (k+1) = x k; reason: the convention is built into prev; same runs
--   - reading: Assumption 1(b) g : E → R ∩ {+∞} → g : E → EReal, proper (never −∞, not identically +∞), lsc, convex; reason: R ∩ {+∞} is empty; the intended codomain is R ∪ {+∞}, as in §2 (p.4)
--   - reading: (9) prints ⟨Lφ(y^k) − ∇f(y^k) + ξ^k, x⟩ → ⟨L∇φ(y^k) − ∇f(y^k) + ξ^k, x⟩; reason: Lφ(y^k) is a number where a vector is needed; the text above Algorithm 1 (∇Lφ(y^k)) and the proof of Theorem 3.1 (L∇φ(y^k)) give the only well-typed reading
--   - reading: β_k is the extrapolation parameter satisfying (8); the proofs on pp.7 and 9 use 0 ≤ β_k < 1 → β_k any real satisfying (8); reason: Algorithm 1 imposes only (8); adding 0 ≤ β_k < 1 would be the weaker reading, and strong convexity of φ with (8) gives the bounds on ‖y^k − x^k‖ the proofs need
--   - reading: (26) prints Lφ(y^k), Lφ(x^{k+1}); (27) prints L(φ(y^k) − φ(x^{k+1})) → L∇φ(y^k), L∇φ(x^{k+1}); L(∇φ(y^k) − ∇φ(x^{k+1})); reason: the printed terms are numbers where vectors are needed; the same terms carry ∇ in (13) and (21), pp.6–7
--   - generalization: stated under Assumptions 1 and 2 → Assumption 1 and differentiability of h only; reason: the derivation uses only that ξ^k = ∇h(x^k); Assumption 2 implies the hypotheses, so the paper's case is included
-- source:
--   Phan, Le Thi, DCA based algorithm with extrapolation for nonconvex nonsmooth optimization, arXiv:2106.04743v1, proof of Theorem 3.2, (26) and (27), p.8

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_Assumption1
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_IsDCAeRun
import Definitions.Def_NonconvexSplitting_Shared_LimitingSubdiff

namespace DCAExtrap

open NonconvexSplitting.Shared

/-- **(26)–(27)** (Phan–Le Thi, arXiv:2106.04743v1, proof of Theorem 3.2, p.8). Let `{x^k}` be
generated by DCAe under Assumption 1, with `h` differentiable, and `y^k` the extrapolated
points. Then for every `k`
(26) `L∇φ(y^k) − ∇f(y^k) + ∇h(x^k) − L∇φ(x^{k+1}) ∈ ∂g(x^{k+1})`, and
(27) `L(∇φ(y^k) − ∇φ(x^{k+1})) + ∇f(x^{k+1}) − ∇f(y^k) + ∇h(x^k) − ∇h(x^{k+1}) ∈ ∂_L F(x^{k+1})`.

**Formalization Note.** The page prints `Lφ(y^k)`, `Lφ(x^{k+1})` in (26) and
`L(φ(y^k) − φ(x^{k+1}))` in (27); these are real numbers where vectors are required, and the
same terms appear with `∇` in the proof of Theorem 3.1 (p.6), so `∇φ` is encoded. `∂g` is the
convex subdifferential and `∂_L` the limiting subdifferential
`NonconvexSplitting.Shared.LimitingSubdiff` of the `EReal`-valued `F`. Of Assumption 2 only the
differentiability of `h` is assumed (it forces `ξ^k = ∇h(x^k)`). -/
theorem eq_26_27 {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] {f : E → ℝ} {g : E → EReal} {h : E → ℝ} {φ : E → ℝ} {L l δ : ℝ}
    {x : ℕ → E} {β : ℕ → ℝ} {ξ : ℕ → E}
    (hA1 : Assumption1 f g h φ L l) (hrun : IsDCAeRun f g h φ L l δ x β ξ)
    (hh : Differentiable ℝ h) (k : ℕ) :
    L • gradient φ (extrap x β k) - gradient f (extrap x β k) + gradient h (x k) -
        L • gradient φ (x (k + 1)) ∈ convexSubdiff g (x (k + 1)) ∧
      L • (gradient φ (extrap x β k) - gradient φ (x (k + 1))) + gradient f (x (k + 1)) -
          gradient f (extrap x β k) + gradient h (x k) - gradient h (x (k + 1)) ∈
        LimitingSubdiff (objF f g h) (x (k + 1)) := by sorry

end DCAExtrap
